WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.218
One account holds 100.

2
00:00:04.218 --> 00:00:09.462
Checkout writes 70, and a moment later the ledger service reads the balance.

3
00:00:09.462 --> 00:00:11.808
Which values may that read return?

4
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.906
Each operation is a bar, from when the request leaves to when the answer comes back.

5
00:00:16.906 --> 00:00:19.788
The store did the work at some instant inside it.

6
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.557
A consistency model says which values a read may return.

7
00:00:23.557 --> 00:00:28.558
Each one asks: could a single copy have produced these results, in some order?

8
00:00:28.558 --> 00:00:30.931
Only the rule for that order changes.

9
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:35.106
Linearizable is the strictest: one copy, in real time.

10
00:00:35.106 --> 00:00:41.579
The write ended at 10 milliseconds and the read began at 20, so the read must return 70.

11
00:00:41.733 --> 00:00:44.912
Say the read hit a lagging follower and returned 100.

12
00:00:44.912 --> 00:00:48.570
It started after the write had finished, and still missed it.

13
00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.009
That's not linearizable.

14
00:00:50.167 --> 00:00:52.801
Sequential consistency drops real time.

15
00:00:52.801 --> 00:00:55.165
Put the read first, then the write.

16
00:00:55.165 --> 00:01:00.704
Each process still sees its own operations in order, so one rung down, it's legal.

17
00:01:00.933 --> 00:01:07.267
To break sequential, add a refund that writes 50 at the same moment as checkout's 70.

18
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:09.427
The ledger reads 70, then 50.

19
00:01:09.427 --> 00:01:11.663
The auditor reads 50, then 70.

20
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:14.396
The ledger's reads say 70 came first.

21
00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:16.106
The auditor's say 50 did.

22
00:01:16.106 --> 00:01:19.524
No single order fits both, so it isn't sequential.

23
00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.513
Causal consistency allows it.

24
00:01:21.513 --> 00:01:26.327
Neither write caused the other, so each reader may put them in its own order.

25
00:01:26.533 --> 00:01:28.811
To break causal, chain the writes.

26
00:01:28.811 --> 00:01:32.228
The ledger reads 70, then writes a fee based on it.

27
00:01:32.228 --> 00:01:36.516
The auditor reads that fee, then reads the balance and gets 100.

28
00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:38.757
The fee exists because of the 70.

29
00:01:38.757 --> 00:01:43.635
The auditor saw the effect, then missed its cause, so this isn't even causal.

30
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:46.101
Eventual consistency still holds.

31
00:01:46.101 --> 00:01:49.948
It only promises that the copies agree once writes stop.

32
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:52.203
Each rung down drops one demand.

33
00:01:52.203 --> 00:01:55.193
Pick the weakest model your code can live with.
